  "prompt_text": "This figure is a simplified map of the brain showing two partially overlapping neural circuits linked to autism-related behavioral domains. A red pathway represents social behaviour, while a blue pathway represents repetitive behaviours and inflexible behavioural preferences. The routes trace connections from frontal forebrain regions through a midline thalamic area labeled VM and into the cerebellum.\n\nThe main visual message is that distinct behaviors are associated with different cerebellar targets, even though the networks share some upstream nodes. Both circuits appear to pass near the same thalamic region, suggesting a common relay point, but then diverge in the cerebellum. The red social-behaviour circuit continues to a lateral cerebellar region labeled Rcrus 1, whereas the blue repetitive/inflexible-behaviour circuit extends toward the posterior vermis. A frontal cortical area labeled PRL is also connected near the top of the diagram.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes a brain-wide organization in which separate but related cerebro-cerebellar pathways may support different aspects of behavior: one more associated with social function and the other with repetitive or rigid behavioral patterns.",
